(window.SITE_PARTS=window.SITE_PARTS||{})["bill-grid"]={"plain":{"bills":{"grid":{"slug":"grid","title":"The American Grid Act","sections":[{"anchor":"sec-1","heading":"\u00a7 1. Short title","part":null,"body":"This Act may be cited as the American Grid Act."},{"anchor":"sec-2","heading":"\u00a7 2. Findings","part":null,"body":"**(1)** More than two thousand gigawatts of proposed generation and storage \u2014 several times the capacity of the existing fleet, and overwhelmingly solar, storage, and wind \u2014 wait in interconnection queues, for years each, while study processes assign single projects network-upgrade costs in the tens or hundreds of millions of dollars. Most withdraw.\n\n**(2)** The Electric Reliability Council of Texas studies only the local facilities needed to connect a new generator and manages congestion afterward through market redispatch. It interconnects more new capacity, faster, than any other system in the United States: 14.2 gigawatts in 2021 and 2022, against 5.6 gigawatts in the largest regional transmission organization, a system with more than 1.8 times its peak load.\n\n**(3)** Where transmission has been planned ahead of need and its cost spread broadly, it was built and the generation followed: Texas's Competitive Renewable Energy Zone lines, more than 3,500 miles, all energized by the end of 2013, and the Midcontinent multi-value portfolio, whose measured benefits exceed twice its costs.\n\n**(4)** Interstate natural gas pipelines have been sited under a single Federal certificate, with eminent domain, since 1938, and the national gas network was built. An interstate electric transmission line enjoys no comparable authority: the Federal siting provision enacted in 2005 was conditioned on corridor designations, and in twenty years not one permit issued under it; the Department of Energy canceled the last three pending corridor designations in August 2026, leaving the authority as it began \u2014 unused. A single interregional line has waited more than a decade in sequential State proceedings.\n\n**(5)** In February 2021, extreme cold collapsed generation and gas supply in a region with limited ability to import power from its neighbors, and more than two hundred people died. Studies by the Department of Energy and the North American Electric Reliability Corporation have since found the value of interregional transfer capability during extreme events to be measured in lives and in billions of dollars per event.\n\n**(6)** The Federal Energy Regulatory Commission's rules on long-term regional planning, interconnection reform, and distributed aggregation \u2014 Orders 1920, 2023, and 2222 \u2014 state sound requirements, and each is a rule: subject to rescission by a later Commission, to abeyance in litigation, and to non-enforcement. The requirements this Act states in statute are those a rule cannot protect.\n\n**(7)** A single capacity auction in the mid-Atlantic rose by more than nine billion dollars on the arrival of forecasted data-center load, and the increase was spread across the bills of every retail customer in the region.\n\n**(8)** Advanced conductors installed on existing towers approximately double the capacity of a line without new structures or new rights-of-way, and reconductoring is routinely excluded from planning models that decide what gets built.\n\n**(9)** Where jurisdictions pay annual impact fees to the communities that host high-voltage lines, as Wisconsin has by statute, lines are built with less opposition; a benefit shared is a fight avoided.\n\n**(10)** Equipment already installed in American homes and businesses \u2014 thermostats, water heaters, batteries, rooftop solar, and electric vehicles \u2014 is aggregated today into between 30 and 60 gigawatts of dispatchable capability. The Department of Energy estimates that 80 to 160 gigawatts of it by 2030 would meet 10 to 20 percent of peak demand and save approximately $10 billion each year in the cost of serving load. The equipment is bought and paid for by the customer; what is scarce is permission to use it.\n\n**(11)** The Commission issued its distributed aggregation rule in September 2020. Six years later, the largest organized market has moved full implementation to February 2028 and the second largest to 2030; the pilot in the largest single-State market raised its registered capacity cap in increments, from 40 megawatts to 500, while the capacity actually participating remained in the tens of megawatts. **The binding constraint on these resources is not their cost, their reliability, or their number. It is the pace at which permission is granted.**\n\n**(12)** In 2024 the California system operator curtailed 3.4 million megawatt-hours of wind and solar generation \u2014 93 percent of it solar, and 29 percent more than the year before. That energy was built, paid for, and discarded.\n\n**(13)** Interconnection on an enforced operating limit, rather than on the worst case a study can imagine, releases capacity on circuits already built: a 15-megawatt solar project interconnected on those terms in New York was curtailed one-tenth of one percent of its annual output.\n\n**(14)** A utility that met load growth in Brooklyn and Queens with demand-side resources rather than a $1.2 billion substation reached its 52-megawatt target in three years and avoided the substation. Its customer-side procurement fell short of that portion's own goal \u2014 33.5 megawatts against 41 \u2014 and utility-side resources made up the difference."},{"anchor":"sec-3","heading":"\u00a7 3. Definitions","part":null,"body":"**(a) Transmission provider.** A **transmission provider** is a public utility, or other entity, that owns, controls, or operates facilities used for the transmission of electric energy, and includes a regional transmission organization and an independent system operator.\n\n**(b) Planning region.** A **planning region** is the geographic footprint within which a transmission provider or group of transmission providers conducts regional transmission planning, as it exists on the date of enactment or as later modified. Two planning regions are **neighboring** if their footprints share a boundary or are connected by an existing transmission tie, **and no other test applies.**\n\n**(c) Covered line.** A **covered line** is a transmission facility of a capacity and voltage not below the floor set in the schedule to this Act that **(1)** crosses a boundary between two States or between two planning regions, or **(2)** is identified in a regional or interregional plan under \u00a7 4 or \u00a7 5 as serving a need of more than one State.\n\n**(d) Interconnection customer.** An **interconnection customer** is a person seeking to interconnect a generation or storage facility to transmission or distribution facilities.\n\n**(e) Energy-only interconnection service.** **Energy-only interconnection service** is interconnection service under which the customer may deliver energy when transmission capacity is available at its node, bears curtailment when it is not, and holds no right to firm delivery.\n\n**(f) Large load.** A **large load** is a single customer, or a group of customers under common control at one site or contiguous sites, with new or expanded demand exceeding the threshold set in the schedule to this Act, **regardless of corporate form, metering arrangement, or characterization, and including a load served through a generating facility interconnected to the grid.**\n\n**(g) Host community.** A **host community** is a county, municipality, or federally recognized Indian Tribe within whose jurisdiction or lands a covered line or a generation or storage facility above the size set in the schedule to this Act is physically located.\n\n**(h) Record complete.** The record on an application is **complete** on the earliest date by which the deciding entity has received the application and every study, document, and fee that a statute or a rule published before the application was filed makes a condition of decision; a request for material not so published does not delay completeness.\n\n**(i) Distributed energy resource.** A **distributed energy resource** is a device or facility, located on a distribution system or behind a retail customer's meter, that is capable of supplying electric energy to the system serving it, of storing electric energy, or of reducing or shifting its consumption of electric energy from a measured baseline, **and includes generation, energy storage, an electric vehicle and its charging equipment, a controllable end use, and any combination of them at one site, without regard to ownership.** **No agency may add to or subtract from this subsection by rule.**\n\n**(j) Aggregation; aggregator.** An **aggregation** is one or more distributed energy resources offered into a wholesale market as a single resource, and may comprise resources of more than one kind, of more than one owner, and at more than one point on a distribution system. An **aggregator** is the person that offers an aggregation. **A retail customer is not a public utility, and is not subject to any provision of Part II of the Federal Power Act, by reason of its resource's participation in an aggregation.**\n\n**(k) Utility.** A **utility** is a person engaged in the distribution or sale of electric energy to the public, including a State or municipal instrumentality and a cooperative."},{"anchor":"sec-4","heading":"\u00a7 4. Planning is a statutory duty","part":null,"body":"**(a) The duty.** Each transmission provider shall participate in a regional transmission plan for its planning region, prepared and published at least every four years, that \u2014\n\n**(1)** assesses transmission needs over a period of not less than twenty years, using plausible scenarios of load growth, generation retirement and addition, extreme weather, and public policy, and not fewer than the number of scenarios set in the schedule to this Act;\n\n**(2)** evaluates, for each identified need, reconductoring with advanced conductors, grid-enhancing technologies, storage as a transmission asset, demand-side resources, including aggregations under \u00a7 11, and interregional solutions, **on the same terms as new construction**, and states in the plan, for each need for which one of those is not selected, the specific reason it does not meet the need \u2014 **a statement that the option was not studied, or that models do not accommodate it, does not satisfy this paragraph**;\n\n**(3)** selects the portfolio of facilities that meets the identified needs at the lowest reasonable long-term cost to consumers, measured across the enumerated benefits in subsection (b); and\n\n**(4)** allocates the cost of each selected facility among the customers of the region in proportion to the benefits stated in subsection (b), **and to no entity that receives none of them.**\n\n**(b) Benefits are enumerated.** The benefits counted in planning and allocation are: reduced production and congestion costs; reduced losses; deferred or avoided facilities; reduced reserve requirements; improved reliability and resilience, including transfer capability during extreme events; reduced curtailment of existing resources; and access to lower-cost generation. **These and only these; an entity's policy disagreement with the generation a line enables is not a cost, and its customers' access to that generation is not a harm.**\n\n**(c) Local projects join the review.** A transmission project above the cost threshold set in the schedule to this Act is included in the regional plan's evaluation whether or not it is labeled local, supplemental, or asset-management, **regardless of characterization.**\n\n**(d) The duty runs without the Commission.** The duties of this section bind each transmission provider directly and are enforceable under \u00a7 14 whether or not the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission has issued, retained, or rescinded any rule on the subject. The Commission may by rule add scenarios, benefits, or process **and may not subtract from this section.**\n\n**(e) Curtailment is measured and published.** Each transmission provider shall publish annually, for each node or the smallest area for which it computes prices, the quantity of electric energy curtailed in the prior year, by resource class and by cause. Publication is a ministerial duty, and mandamus lies to compel it. **Where curtailment at a node exceeds the threshold set in the schedule to this Act, the constraint producing it is an identified need in the next plan under subsection (a)**, to be met by any of the options subsection (a)(2) requires be evaluated."},{"anchor":"sec-4a","heading":"\u00a7 4A. Ratings that reflect conditions","part":null,"body":"**(a) The rating.** Each transmission provider shall compute, and use in every determination of available capacity, a rating for each transmission line over which it provides service that reflects forecast ambient air temperature and, where they limit the line, solar heating and wind, updated not less frequently than hourly for each hour of the period set in the schedule to this Act. **A rating that assumes a fixed ambient condition may not be used where this subsection supplies one.**\n\n**(a-1) What satisfies subsection (a).** A transmission provider that computes and uses ratings under a rule of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission requiring not less than subsection (a) requires **satisfies subsection (a) by doing so**, and no separate computation, filing, or showing is required of it.\n\n**(b) Measured conditions where congestion is measured.** For each line whose congestion cost in the prior year exceeded the threshold set in the schedule to this Act, the transmission provider shall additionally compute and use a rating that reflects conditions measured on or at the line, within the period set in the schedule to this Act after the year in which the threshold was exceeded. **Where the transmission provider does not compute a congestion cost, this subsection applies instead to each line operated at or above its rating for more than the number of hours set in the schedule to this Act.**\n\n**(c) Published.** Each rating under this section, the inputs used, and the method are published as they are computed. Publication is a ministerial duty, and mandamus lies to compel it.\n\n**(d) One way.** The Federal Energy Regulatory Commission may by rule require ratings that reflect conditions more closely, more frequently, or on more lines, **and may not require less, and may not exempt any line or any transmission provider.**\n\n**(e) Reliability standards are not displaced.** A rating under this section is computed within the equipment ratings and the rating methodology that a reliability standard under section 215 of the Federal Power Act establishes, and **this section changes no such standard and authorizes the operation of no facility beyond a limit one establishes**, and no such standard is a ground for declining to compute, publish, or use a rating this section requires. The threshold of subsection (b) is a step: a line above it is subject to that subsection in full."},{"anchor":"sec-5","heading":"\u00a7 5. Interregional transfer capability","part":null,"body":"**(a) The requirement.** Each pair of neighboring planning regions shall achieve and maintain the ability to transfer between them, in each direction, electric energy equal to the lesser of \u2014 **(1)** 30 percent of the higher of the two regions' peak demands; or **(2)** the transfer capability existing on the date of enactment plus 15 percent of that higher peak demand.\n\n**(b) The glide path.** The requirement of subsection (a) is met not later than the tenth anniversary of enactment, with not less than half of the required increase in service by the sixth anniversary. Compliance may be achieved by new lines, upgrades, reconductoring, grid-enhancing technologies, high-voltage direct current ties, or any combination.\n\n**(c) The joint plan.** Within two years after enactment, the transmission providers of each pair of neighboring planning regions shall file with the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission a joint plan identifying the facilities, schedule, and cost allocation by which they will comply. Costs are allocated between the regions in proportion to the subsection (d) benefits to each, and within each region under \u00a7 4(a)(4).\n\n**(d) Measurement.** Transfer capability, peak demand, and compliance are measured by the methods of the North American Electric Reliability Corporation's interregional transfer capability study as they exist on the date of enactment, as tightened \u2014 **and not loosened** \u2014 by any later rule.\n\n**(e) Default operation.** The requirement of subsection (a) applies of its own force. Where a joint plan is not filed by the deadline of subsection (c), or the Commission has issued no implementing rule, the requirement is enforceable under \u00a7 14 against each transmission provider of the deficient pair, and the district court shall order the filing of a compliant joint plan on a schedule not exceeding 180 days.\n\n**(f) Jurisdictional boundary preserved.** This section applies to a planning region not otherwise subject to Part II of the Federal Power Act, but neither this section nor compliance with it subjects such a region, or any entity within it, to any other provision of Part II, **and the omission of any broader jurisdictional consequence is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**"},{"anchor":"sec-6","heading":"\u00a7 6. Interconnection","part":null,"body":"**(a) The default regime.** For an interconnection request electing energy-only interconnection service, the study of the request is limited to \u2014 **(1)** the interconnection facilities connecting the customer to the point of interconnection; and **(2)** a reliability screen confined to protection, stability, and short-circuit effects at and near that point. **No network-upgrade study, and no assignment of network-upgrade costs, is a condition of energy-only service.** Congestion arising from the interconnection is managed by redispatch and curtailment priced at the customer's node.\n\n**(b) Firm service.** An interconnection customer may obtain firm delivery rights by funding, or having funded under \u00a7 8(c), the network upgrades a study identifies. A customer taking energy-only service may elect firm service later, with the same priority as a new request for the upgrade study only.\n\n**(c) Study clocks.** Each study under this section is complete within the period set in the schedule to this Act. Where a study is not delivered within its period, **the study of record is the customer's own study, performed at the customer's cost by a licensed professional engineer meeting the qualifications set in the schedule to this Act and sworn by that engineer, and the transmission provider shall interconnect on its basis** unless within 60 days it delivers its own study identifying, with specificity, a protection, stability, or short-circuit constraint the independent study missed.\n\n**(d) Existing points of interconnection.** The interconnection rights of a generating facility that retires transfer, at the retiring owner's election and at the same point, to a replacement generation or storage facility, without a new queue position, subject only to the subsection (a)(2) screen. Surplus capacity at any existing point of interconnection is available on the same terms.\n\n**(e) Readiness.** A transmission provider may require deposits and site control before studying a request, at levels set in the schedule to this Act, **and may not require more.**\n\n**(f) Interconnection on an enforced limit.** An interconnection customer may elect interconnection subject to a limit on injection, on withdrawal, or on both, enforced by an automatic control that the customer installs at the customer's cost, that operates without any act of the customer, and that the transmission provider or utility may monitor and test. **The study of a request so electing is limited to operation at the elected limit**, and no network upgrade may be required as a condition of that service where operation at the limit violates no protection, stability, or short-circuit constraint at or near the point of interconnection. **The grounds on which service under this subsection may be refused or limited are those and only those stated in this subsection, and the omission of any other ground is a determination of Congress and not a gap.** **An election under this subsection may not be refused**, and this subsection applies to a request to interconnect to distribution facilities as it applies to a request to interconnect to transmission facilities. Where the transmission provider or utility determines that a limit lower than the one elected is required, it shall state, with specificity, the facility and the constraint requiring it; **a statement identifying no facility and no constraint is not a determination.** The control may be monitored and tested at the provider's cost and on notice to the customer.\n\n**(f-1) The election is the customer's alone.** A transmission provider or utility **may not require an election under subsection (f), condition any service, study, or queue position on one, or offer service under subsection (f) as the only service available**, and a term of an agreement to the contrary is void.\n\n**(g) What the limit costs, measured.** For each facility interconnected under subsection (f), the transmission provider or utility publishes annually the hours and the energy the control curtailed. **Where curtailment in a year exceeds the fraction of the facility's output set in the schedule to this Act, the customer may elect energy-only or firm service under subsections (a) and (b) without a new queue position**, and the constraint producing the curtailment is an identified need in the next plan under \u00a7 4(a)."},{"anchor":"sec-7","heading":"\u00a7 7. Federal siting for covered lines","part":null,"body":"**(b) Amendment.** Section 216 of the Federal Power Act (16 U.S.C. 824p) is amended so that, for a covered line \u2014\n\n> **(1)** the Commission may issue a permit for the construction or modification of the line **on application, without regard to whether the line is within any designated corridor**, on findings that the line is a covered line; that it will be used for the transmission of electric energy in interstate commerce; that it will significantly increase transfer capability or reduce congestion, or is selected in a plan under section 4 or 5 of the American Grid Act; that its route maximizes the use of existing rights-of-way and previously disturbed land to the extent practicable; and that the applicant has made the good faith efforts to engage landowners and other stakeholders that this section already requires;\n>\n> **(2)** a permit so issued is the single Federal authorization for the siting of the line; **no approval of a State or political subdivision is a condition of construction or operation of the permitted line**, and the standards of this paragraph supersede State siting law for the permitted line, expressly and to that extent only; and\n>\n> **(3)** the holder may exercise the right of eminent domain in the manner and on the conditions this section already provides, as a last resort after the good-faith-engagement requirement is satisfied, with compensation at fair market value plus severance damages, and the right-of-way terminates if the line ceases to be used.\n\n**(c) Clocks and review.** Sections 5, 6, and 7 of the Clean Energy Speed Act apply to an application under this section, and if that Act is not in effect, those sections apply as they read on the date of enactment of this Act.\n\n**(d) Operative in any event.** With respect to a covered line, paragraphs (1) through (3) of subsection (b) operate according to their terms whether or not the amendment made by subsection (b) takes effect."},{"anchor":"sec-8","heading":"\u00a7 8. Enabling finance","part":null,"body":"**(a) The chassis, scaled.** The authorities of section 40106 of the Infrastructure Investment and Jobs Act (42 U.S.C. 18713) \u2014 capacity contracts, loans, and public-private partnerships for eligible transmission projects \u2014 are made permanent; the limitation on outstanding repayable balances is raised to the amount set in the schedule to this Act; and a covered line is an eligible project without further designation.\n\n**(b) Anchor tenant.** In exercising the capacity-contract authority for a covered line, the Secretary of Energy shall contract for capacity sufficient to permit financing **where the line is selected in a plan under \u00a7 4 or \u00a7 5 \u2014 a fact established by the plan's publication, on which no finding of the Secretary is required** \u2014 and shall terminate or assign the contracted capacity as subscribers arrive, as section 40106 provides.\n\n**(c) Network-upgrade front-funding.** From the fund of section 40106, the Secretary shall pay, at the election of a transmission provider or an interconnection customer, the cost of network upgrades identified for firm service under \u00a7 6(b). Amounts so paid are recovered through transmission rates over a period not exceeding thirty years under the allocation of \u00a7 4(a)(4), and the recovery is remitted to the fund. **An election under this subsection may not be refused, conditioned, or delayed by any entity other than for exhaustion of the fund, and exhaustion is published the day it occurs.**\n\n**(d) Household electrification finance.** Each utility selling electric energy at retail shall offer a tariff under which a customer, including a tenant with the account, may repay the cost of a heat pump, heat-pump water heater, electric panel or wiring upgrade, rooftop or plug-in solar system, or storage battery through the bill for the meter served, on these terms and no others less favorable: the periodic charge does not exceed the estimated bill savings from the improvement, computed by the published method set in the schedule to this Act; the obligation attaches to the meter and transfers with the account at the premises; **no credit score, credit history, or income verification may be required or considered**; and the charge is suspended, without arrears, for any period the meter is disconnected. **The charge is a charge for utility service under a tariff; it is not an extension of credit, creates no debt of the customer, and the Truth in Lending Act does not apply to it.** Capital for the tariff is available to the utility from the fund of section 40106 at the Treasury rate plus administration, in the manner of that section's loans, **and the administration component may not exceed the rate set in the schedule to this Act.** Priority in marketing and deployment is for households above the energy-burden threshold set in the schedule to this Act.\n\n**(d-1) Industrial heat.** Capital is available from the fund of section 40106, at the Treasury rate plus administration capped as subsection (d) provides, to finance thermal energy storage, electric boilers, industrial heat pumps, and the interconnection and electrical upgrades they require, at any manufacturing facility. The periodic repayment may not exceed the fuel-cost savings measured by the published method set in the schedule to this Act; the obligation attaches to the facility and transfers with it; and each financed installation shall be capable of, and its retail service shall provide for, drawing its energy preferentially in the hours of lowest wholesale prices. **No credit score or rating of the facility's owner may be required or considered**; the collateral is the savings stream and the installation.\n\n**(e) Supply chain.** The Secretary of Energy may enter into advance purchase commitments and offtake guarantees, in the manner of title III of the Defense Production Act of 1950, for large power transformers, high-voltage direct current equipment, advanced conductors, and grid-scale storage manufactured in the United States, within the annual limit set in the schedule to this Act.\n\n**(e-1) The standing offer.** For each equipment class listed in the schedule to this Act, the Secretary publishes a performance standard and a standing offer: **equipment manufactured in the United States that a nationally recognized testing laboratory certifies to the standard is purchasable by the United States, at the published price and up to the published annual volume, on tender** \u2014 and the offer, once published for a class, may be revised only prospectively and only after the notice period set in the schedule to this Act. Equipment so purchased is resold, leased, or allocated to transmission providers and utilities at cost. **A tender of certified equipment may not be refused within the published volume, and no finding, ranking, or discretion of any officer is a condition of purchase.**\n\n**(e-1B) The Strategic Grid Reserve.** Equipment purchased under subsection (e-1) and not yet resold or allocated constitutes the **Strategic Grid Reserve**, which the Secretary maintains at not less than the minimum inventory per class set in the schedule to this Act, stored ready for transport. Reserve equipment is released, at cost and with priority by order of request, **on the criteria set in the schedule to this Act \u2014 including the failure, destruction, or compelled removal from service of comparable equipment \u2014 and release on those criteria is ministerial: no declaration, finding, or discretion of any officer is a condition of it, and mandamus lies to compel it.** Equipment released is replaced through the standing offer, and the Secretary publishes the reserve's inventory, by class, quarterly.\n\n**(e-1A) Clean materials.** For each near-zero-emission materials class listed in the schedule to this Act \u2014 including structural steel and cement \u2014 the Secretary publishes a maximum carbon intensity per unit, computed as the Clean Industry Act computes intensity (and if that Act is not in effect, by the method there stated as introduced), and a standing offer at a published premium and annual volume. **Material produced in the United States whose intensity a verified environmental product declaration certifies at or below the maximum is purchasable by the United States on tender, and a tender may not be refused within the published volume; no finding, ranking, or discretion of any officer is a condition of purchase.** Material so purchased is allocated into Federal and federally assisted projects through the procurement of section 14 of the Fair Energy Market Act, and the offer may be revised only prospectively, after the notice period of subsection (e-1).\n\n**(e-2) Prizes.** The Secretary may administer prize competitions under section 24 of the Stevenson-Wydler Technology Innovation Act of 1980 (15 U.S.C. 3719) for grid equipment meeting performance targets the Secretary publishes, within the annual limit set in the schedule to this Act; a prize determination is final and creates no continuing obligation.\n\n**(f) Character.** A loan or front-funding under this section is a loan, not income, to the recipient; recoveries and repayments are not gross income to the fund's account holders; and a payment under a capacity contract or offtake guarantee is an amount realized by the counterparty in the ordinary course."},{"anchor":"sec-9","heading":"\u00a7 9. Large loads pay their way","part":null,"body":"**(a) Filed contract.** A large load may be served only under a written contract filed with the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission or the State commission with jurisdiction, and published with confidential pricing redacted no further than the schedule to this Act permits.\n\n**(b) Cost causation.** The contract shall recover from the large load the incremental transmission, distribution, and capacity costs its service requires, computed by a published method, **and no part of those incremental costs may be allocated to other customers.** A contribution the large load makes to costs that would exist without it is unaffected.\n\n**(c) Flexibility counts.** Incremental costs under subsection (b) are computed net of the large load's binding commitments to curtail or shift demand, enforceable under the contract.\n\n**(d) Anti-substitution.** This section applies to service arranged through any structure \u2014 behind-the-meter generation interconnected to the grid, affiliate resale, or otherwise \u2014 **regardless of form, title, or characterization.** A large load served entirely by facilities not interconnected to the grid is outside this section, and that omission is a determination of Congress."},{"anchor":"sec-9a","heading":"\u00a7 9A. Large loads run clean","part":null,"body":"**(a) New large loads.** A large load commencing service after the date of enactment shall retire, for each compliance year, clean energy credits \u2014 as defined in, and through the registries of, the Clean, Honest Utilities Act, and if that Act is not in effect, as those provisions read on the date of enactment of this Act \u2014 equal to 100 percent of its consumption of electric energy for that year, from whatever source supplied. Credits retired under this subsection are in addition to, and may not also be counted toward, any utility's own compliance.\n\n**(b) Existing large loads.** A large load in service on the date of enactment reaches the requirement of subsection (a) on the schedule set in the schedule to this Act, rising each year and reaching 100 percent by the date there set.\n\n**(c) The fence provides no exit.** A generating facility that combusts or otherwise chemically converts a carbon-bearing fuel and serves a large load \u2014 whether or not interconnected to the grid, and **regardless of form, title, ownership, or characterization** \u2014 shall cease that service by the date set in the schedule to this Act, unless converted to operate without such combustion or conversion; and energy a large load consumes from such a facility before that date counts as consumption under subsections (a) and (b) and earns no credit.\n\n**(d) Water.** A large load using water for cooling shall operate at or below the water-use effectiveness limit set in the schedule to this Act, measured annually; the limit may be tightened by rule and may not be loosened; and a large load in service on the date of enactment complies by the date set in the schedule to this Act.\n\n**(e) Neighbors.** A large load shall not cause, at the nearest existing dwelling, sound exceeding the levels set in the schedule to this Act, by day and by night; the limit is measured, not modeled; and a person residing at an affected dwelling may enforce this subsection under \u00a7 14 as a person aggrieved.\n\n**(f) Publication.** Each large load files annually, and the Commission publishes, its consumption, credits retired, water use and effectiveness, and measured sound levels. The filing is sworn; a knowingly false filing is punishable under section 1001 of title 18, United States Code; and a shortfall of credits under subsection (a) or (b) is a debt computed at the alternative compliance payment of the Clean, Honest Utilities Act, or, if that Act is not in effect, at the amount set in the schedule to this Act, recoverable with a civil penalty determined by a district court.\n\n**(g) Severability within this section.** Subsections (a) through (f) are severable from one another, and the application of each to any class of large load is severable from its application to every other class."},{"anchor":"sec-10","heading":"\u00a7 10. Host communities","part":null,"body":"**(a) The payments.** The owner of a covered line, or of a generation or storage facility above the size set in the schedule to this Act, shall pay to each host community \u2014 **(1)** once, on commencement of construction, the percentage of the capital cost allocated to that community's jurisdiction set in the schedule to this Act; and **(2)** annually, the percentage set in the schedule to this Act. Payments are computed from the same capital cost stated for rate purposes, are recoverable in rates as a cost of the facility, and are apportioned among host communities by route-miles or acreage.\n\n**(b) Published, uniform, exclusive.** The rates of subsection (a) are uniform nationally and are not subject to negotiation, waiver, or supplementation project by project. **A State or political subdivision may not condition any approval, service, or cooperation respecting a covered line on any payment, exaction, or in-kind contribution beyond this section**, and a demand to the contrary is ineffective.\n\n**(c) Tribal lands.** Nothing in this section limits the terms a federally recognized Indian Tribe may set for facilities on its trust or reservation lands, and subsection (b)'s limitation does not apply to them."},{"anchor":"sec-11","heading":"\u00a7 11. Distributed resources, demand flexibility, and honest prices","part":null,"body":"**(a) Aggregation.** A distributed energy resource aggregation of a capacity not below the floor set in the schedule to this Act may participate in each wholesale market operated by a transmission provider, on terms comparable to other resources of comparable capability, and no State or utility may prohibit a resource's participation through an aggregator. **A State may, by law of general application, elect that aggregations of resources on distribution systems of small utilities, as defined in the schedule to this Act, not participate**, and no other restriction is effective. A requirement that the resources of an aggregation be of a single technology, be owned by one person, be served by one utility, or be located behind one meter **is not effective**, except as the market applies the same locational requirement to every other resource.\n\n**(a-1) The utility is not a gatekeeper.** A utility may review a resource's participation in an aggregation **only for the physical effects of the resource's operation on the distribution system**, on the standard and within the period that govern an interconnection review of that resource under \u00a7 6 or under \u00a7 13 of the Clean Energy Speed Act, and a review on any other ground **is not effective**. A review shall identify the specific facility affected and the specific effect; **a review identifying neither is not a review**, and the period for it runs from the utility's receipt of notice of the resource's participation. A utility may not require that it, or an affiliate of it, be the aggregator; may not condition retail service, any rate, or any interconnection on the customer's choice of aggregator or on the customer's enrollment or non-enrollment in any program; and a term of service to the contrary **is void.**\n\n**(a-2) A retail program is not a bar.** A resource enrolled in a program of its utility or of a State may also participate in an aggregation, **and the enrollment is not a ground on which participation may be refused, limited, or conditioned.** Compensation for the same measured performance in the same hour is payable once: the market operator and the utility net their payments by the published method of the market operator, **and that netting is the whole of the remedy \u2014 exclusion from either is not.** **Where no such method is published, the utility's payment for that hour is reduced by the amount the market operator paid for the same measured performance, by operation of this subsection and without any further act of any person**, and no other adjustment is made.\n\n**(a-3) Measurement, and no more than comparable.** Metering, telemetry, testing, certification, and data requirements applied to an aggregation or to its resources **may not exceed, per unit of capability, those applied to other resources of comparable capability.** Measurement at the aggregation \u2014 by meters at its resources, or by a statistically valid sample of them under a published sampling method \u2014 satisfies any requirement that its performance be measured, and **a requirement of dedicated communications equipment at each resource is not effective** where the aggregation's performance is so measurable. Each baseline method is published before the period it measures, **and measures performance against consumption in comparable hours not affected by a dispatch.**\n\n**(a-4) Accreditation on measured performance.** The capacity value of an aggregation is determined by its measured performance in the hours in which the region's reliability need is greatest, **by the method applied to every other resource.** No reduction, discount, or exclusion applies to an aggregation by reason of its technology, its connection at distribution, or its composition. **A limit on the quantity of aggregation capacity that may register, participate, clear, or be accredited is not effective**, and a pilot, phase, or transitional program is not a ground for one. **Nothing in this subsection limits a performance requirement, a testing requirement, or a penalty for nonperformance that applies to every other resource of comparable capability.** A method that produces a capacity value not derived from the aggregation's measured performance is not the method this subsection requires.\n\n**(a-5) What does not qualify.** A reduction in consumption achieved by operating a facility that combusts or otherwise chemically converts a carbon-bearing fuel **is not a reduction for any purpose of this section**, and energy supplied by such a facility earns no payment as a distributed energy resource, **regardless of form, title, ownership, or characterization.**\n\n**(b) The customer's own data.** Each utility selling electric energy at retail shall make available to each customer, and to each person the customer authorizes, **without charge and in a structured, machine-readable form**, the customer's metered energy and demand data at the interval at which the meter records it, within the period set in the schedule to this Act and on a continuing basis while the authorization stands. The utility shall accept an authorization and a revocation in electronic form, and a revocation takes effect within the period set in the schedule to this Act. **A utility may not condition an authorization on the identity of the person authorized, on that person's agreement to any term with the utility, or on any fee.** A person receiving data under this subsection may use it only for the purpose the customer authorized; with respect to that data the person is subject to the Personal Privacy Act, and if that Act is not in effect, to its provisions as they read on the date of enactment of this Act. **This subsection authorizes no transfer of data to a person the customer has not authorized.** Where a meter does not record at intervals, the utility makes available the data the meter does record; **this subsection requires the installation of no meter, and a utility may not lengthen the interval at which its meters record, or discontinue their recording, in order to limit what this subsection requires.** **A customer, or a person the customer authorized, to whom data is not made available as this subsection requires is a person aggrieved for purposes of \u00a7 14.**\n\n**(c) Vehicles are resources.** An electric vehicle and its charging equipment, composed of equipment listed by a nationally recognized testing laboratory to the standard governing that equipment, interconnect and participate **on the terms applicable to an energy storage facility of comparable capability**, and a utility may not require any additional agreement, meter, study, or fee by reason of the resource being a vehicle. **Supply from a vehicle to the premises at which it is connected, through equipment that cannot energize the utility's system, requires no permit, application, agreement, approval, fee, meter change, or notice**, and a term of service to the contrary is void.\n\n**(d) Reconsideration.** Each State regulatory authority and each nonregulated electric utility shall, within two years after enactment, complete a new consideration, under section 111(d) of the Public Utility Regulatory Policies Act of 1978, of the standards stated in paragraphs (14), (19), (20), and (21) of that subsection, on a record that includes the changes in generation mix, storage cost, and midday wholesale prices since its last consideration, and shall publish its determination and the reasons for it. **A determination that adopts or declines a standard without addressing that record does not satisfy this subsection.**\n\n**(e) New standards.** Section 111(d) of the Public Utility Regulatory Policies Act of 1978 (16 U.S.C. 2621(d)) is amended by adding at the end the following:\n\n> **\"(22) Solar-hour default rates.** Each electric utility shall make available to each customer class, as the default rate schedule for new service commenced after the consideration of this paragraph is complete, a time-varying rate under which the price of energy in the hours of lowest wholesale prices, including hours of abundant solar generation, reflects those prices, and each customer may elect any other offered schedule at any time without charge.\n>\n> **\"(23) Demand flexibility available to every customer.** Each electric utility shall make available to each customer class a program, open to the customer directly and to an aggregator the customer authorizes, under which the customer is compensated at a published price for reducing or shifting consumption, or for supplying energy from a device on the customer's premises, in the hours in which the utility's system is most constrained, and under which enrollment and withdrawal are without charge.\n>\n> **\"(24) Charging that follows the price.** Each electric utility shall make available, for electric vehicle charging, a rate schedule under which the price of energy varies by time of day, and shall permit an electric vehicle and its charging equipment to supply energy to the premises at which the vehicle is connected.\"\n\n**(e-1) Character of payments.** A payment to a residential customer for a reduction in the customer's consumption, or for energy supplied from a distributed energy resource at the customer's residence, **is a reduction of the price the customer pays for electric service, and is not gross income, to the extent the payments so made to that customer in a taxable year do not exceed the amounts the customer paid for electric service in that year**; the excess is gross income. Amounts received by an aggregator are gross income in the ordinary course, and the netting required by subsection (a-2) is taken into account in determining the amount received. **No return of information is required under section 6041 of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 with respect to an amount that is not gross income under this subsection.**\n\n**(f) Procedure.** The consideration of the standards added by subsection (e) proceeds under sections 111(b), 112, and 121 of that Act as for the standards added in 2021, with the times running from the date of enactment of this Act."},{"anchor":"sec-11a","heading":"\u00a7 11A. Demand-side alternatives to distribution investment","part":null,"body":"**(a) The condition.** A utility may not recover in any rate, Federal or State, the cost of a distribution facility built to serve load growth or to relieve a capacity constraint, where that cost exceeds the threshold set in the schedule to this Act, unless \u2014\n\n**(1)** it first published the constraint, its location, the year in which it is projected to bind, and the quantity of relief needed, and solicited offers to meet that need from any resource, **including demand flexibility, aggregations under \u00a7 11, energy storage, distributed generation, and efficiency**, in technology-neutral terms;\n\n**(2)** every offer, and its own proposal, were evaluated on net present cost to customers by the method published with the solicitation; and\n\n**(3)** an independent evaluator, selected as \u00a7 5 of the Clean, Honest Utilities Act provides and from the roster that section keeps, **certified in writing that the solicitation was open, that the evaluation followed the published method, and that the facility prevailed on net present cost.** A certification that recites conclusions without stating the comparison it rests on does not satisfy this paragraph.\n\n**(a-1) What the section reaches.** A facility is built to serve load growth or to relieve a capacity constraint where the utility's own planning, filing, or accounting documents identify either among the reasons for it, **or where the facility increases the capacity of a distribution circuit, substation, or transformer**; and a facility outside this subsection is not within this section.\n\n**(a-2) The threshold is a step.** A cost above the threshold of subsection (a) is subject to this section in its entirety, and no part of a cost is exempt by reason of the portion below the threshold.\n\n**(b) The consequence, and only this consequence.** A cost that fails subsection (a) is not recoverable in any rate, **and the fact of non-recoverability is determined by the absence of the certification, without any inquiry into prudence.** Nothing in this section forbids the construction of the facility itself, at shareholder expense. **An amount collected in a rate in violation of this section is an overcharge, refundable to customers with interest, on the action of a State regulatory authority, the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission, or a customer.**\n\n**(c) Where that Act is not in effect, and the qualifications.** Where \u00a7 5 of the Clean, Honest Utilities Act is not in effect, the selection and the roster of an independent evaluator are those that section states, and its qualifications are those item S-9 of the schedule to that Act sets, each as it read on the date of enactment of this Act, **and the absence of a roster does not excuse subsection (a)(3)**: any person meeting those qualifications and engaged by the State regulatory authority is an independent evaluator for purposes of this section. **Wherever this section applies the qualifications of item S-9, paragraph (4) of that item reads \"distribution facilities, or the resources that may meet a distribution need in their place,\" in place of \"electric generation\".**\n\n**(d) Anti-substitution.** This section applies to a facility **regardless of form, title, or characterization** \u2014 as maintenance, replacement, resiliency, modernization, hardening, or a program \u2014 where its purpose or effect is to serve load growth or to relieve a capacity constraint, and to a facility divided into parts each below the threshold. A utility that recovers such a cost under any other authority shall publish a statement identifying both authorities and the difference between their conditions. **The purpose or effect of a facility is determined by what the facility does, and the utility's characterization of it in any filing, plan, or account is not determinative.**\n\n**(d-1) Whose duty this is.** This section imposes no duty on any State regulatory authority and requires no proceeding, rule, or determination of one. **The duty is the utility's, and the approval of a cost by a State regulatory authority does not make a cost recoverable that subsection (a) makes unrecoverable.**\n\n**(e) What this section does not reach.** This section does not apply to a facility installed to restore service after the failure, destruction, or forced outage of a facility, or to a constraint projected to bind within the period set in the schedule to this Act, **provided that the utility publishes, before recovery, the ground on which the exception is claimed.** **The exceptions to this section are those and only those stated in this subsection, and the omission of any other exception is a determination of Congress and not a gap.**"},{"anchor":"sec-12","heading":"\u00a7 12. Storage","part":null,"body":"**(a)** A storage facility may provide transmission service, and be compensated as a transmission asset, while also participating in markets, under an accounting separation that prevents recovery of the same cost twice; and no rule may require a storage facility to elect a single function.\n\n**(b) Energy is not charged for twice.** Electric energy delivered to an energy storage facility and afterward delivered back to a transmission or distribution system **is not a retail sale to the extent it is so returned**, and no charge for transmission or distribution service may be assessed on it both when it is delivered to the facility and when it is returned. **The quantity so exempt is the metered energy returned, and no other method of computing it applies.** This subsection applies to a storage facility wherever connected, including one behind a retail customer's meter."},{"anchor":"sec-13","heading":"\u00a7 13. No off switch","part":null,"body":"**(a)** Every duty in this Act binds the person it names without any rulemaking, designation, or other agency action, on the defaults this Act supplies. No agency may exempt any person or class from this Act, and every delegated authority in this Act runs one way: to add scenarios, tighten measurements, shorten clocks, or raise floors, and never the reverse.\n\n**(b) Resources and continuity.** There are permanently appropriated such sums as are necessary for the administration of \u00a7\u00a7 5, 7, and 8 and for the processing of applications and studies subject to this Act's clocks. A lapse in appropriations suspends no clock, no deemed outcome, and no duty of publication. **The officers and employees necessary to perform the processing of applications and studies subject to this Act's clocks and the publication duties of this Act shall be designated as excepted from furlough during a lapse in appropriations, identified as such in the published shutdown plan of the agency concerned.**"},{"anchor":"sec-14","heading":"\u00a7 14. Enforcement","part":null,"body":"**(a)** A duty of a transmission provider or utility under this Act is enforceable as if it were a requirement of Part II of the Federal Power Act, by the Commission or by a person aggrieved, and, for a region within \u00a7 5(f), by civil action of a person aggrieved in a district court of the United States, **without thereby subjecting the region to any other provision of Part II.**\n\n**(b)** A duty of publication under this Act is ministerial, and mandamus lies to compel it. The deemed outcomes of \u00a7\u00a7 6(c) and 8(c) operate by law and require no order.\n\n**(c)** No claim under this Act is subject to exhaustion of any administrative remedy not stated in this Act.\n\n**(d) Defense of this Act.** In any action in which the validity or constitutionality of this Act, or of any application of it, is drawn in question and the United States has declined, or has failed after notice from the court, to defend it, **any State, and any person whom the provision in question protects or benefits, may intervene as of right to defend it**, and the court shall not enter judgment against the provision's validity without hearing an intervenor so appearing."},{"anchor":"sec-15","heading":"\u00a7 15. Relationship to other law","part":null,"body":"**(a)** This Act does not displace a State law, or a rule of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission, that plans further ahead, requires more transfer capability, interconnects faster, pays host communities more, or reaches more distributed participation than this Act, and such a law or rule governs to that extent.\n\n**(b)** Except as \u00a7\u00a7 7(b)(2), 10(b), 11(a) through (a-4), 11(b), 11(c), 11A and 12(b) state, this Act preempts no State law, and the omission of any broader preemption is a determination of Congress. **Congress states expressly its intent that each provision named in this subsection supersede the law of the States and their political subdivisions to the extent and only to the extent that provision states, and no presumption against preemption applies to the construction of those provisions.**"},{"anchor":"sec-16","heading":"\u00a7 16. Severability","part":null,"body":"**(a)** If any provision of this Act, or its application to any person or circumstance, is held invalid, the remainder and every other application remain in force.\n\n**(b)** Sections 4, 4A, 5 through 11, 11A, and 12 are severable from one another. Within \u00a7\u00a7 11 and 11A each subsection is severable from every other, and the application of \u00a7 11(b) and of \u00a7 11A to a utility owned by a State or political subdivision is severable from their application to every other utility. Within \u00a7 7, the permit authority of subsection (b)(1) is severable from the preemption of (b)(2) and from the eminent domain provision of (b)(3), and each survives the others' invalidity. Within \u00a7 8, each subsection is severable, and the application of subsection (d) to a utility owned by a State or political subdivision is severable from its application to every other utility. Section 5's application to regions within \u00a7 5(f) is severable from its application to every other region."},{"anchor":"sec-17","heading":"\u00a7 17. Effective dates","part":null,"body":"**(a)** This Act takes effect on the date of enactment. The first plans under \u00a7 4 are published within three years; the \u00a7 8(d) tariff is offered within the period set in the schedule to this Act; and \u00a7 9 applies to contracts executed after the date of enactment.\n\n**(b)** An interconnection request pending on the date of enactment may, at the customer's election, proceed under \u00a7 6 with its existing queue position, and the periods of \u00a7 6(c) run from the election.\n\n**(c)** Section 4A(a) applies, and \u00a7 11(b) data is available, within the periods set in the schedule to this Act. **Section 11A applies to a facility for which the utility first commits capital after the period set in the schedule to this Act has run from the date of enactment**, and to no earlier facility."},{"anchor":"schedule","heading":"SCHEDULE","part":null,"body":"1. **Covered-line capacity and voltage floor** (\u00a7 3(c)): 500 MW; 230 kV\n2. **Large-load threshold** (\u00a7 3(f)): 20 MW\n3. **Host-community facility size floor** (\u00a7 3(g)): [___ MW]\n4. **Minimum planning scenarios** (\u00a7 4(a)(1)): [___ scenarios]\n5. **Local-project review threshold** (\u00a7 4(c)): [$___]\n6. **Interconnection study periods** (\u00a7 6(c)): [___ days, by study class]\n7. **Interconnection deposits and site control** (\u00a7 6(e)): [$___ per MW; site-control instruments]\n8. **Fund borrowing limitation** (\u00a7 8(a)): [$___]\n9. **Bill-savings computation method** (\u00a7 8(d)): [reserved for the drafting committee]\n10. **Energy-burden priority threshold** (\u00a7 8(d)): [___ percent of household income]\n11. **Supply-chain annual commitment limit** (\u00a7 8(e)): [$___ per year]\n12. **Large-load contract redaction limits** (\u00a7 9(a)): [reserved for the drafting committee]\n13. **Host-community payment rates** (\u00a7 10(a)): [___ percent one-time; ___ percent annual]\n14. **Aggregation capacity floor and small-utility definition** (\u00a7 11(a)): [___ kW; ___ GWh annual sales]\n15. **Tariff offering deadline** (\u00a7 17(a)): [___ months after enactment]\n16. **Tariff administration-component cap** (\u00a7 8(d)): [___ basis points]\n17. **Independent-engineer qualifications** (\u00a7 6(c)): [reserved for the drafting committee]\n18. **Standing-offer equipment classes, prices, volumes, and revision notice** (\u00a7 8(e-1)): [reserved for the drafting committee]\n18A. **Reserve minimum inventories and release criteria** (\u00a7 8(e-1B)): [___ units per class; criteria reserved for the drafting committee]\n19. **Prize annual limit** (\u00a7 8(e-2)): [$___ per year]\n20. **Existing large-load clean schedule and terminal date** (\u00a7 9A(b)): [rising schedule; 100 percent by ___]\n21. **Fence-side fossil cessation date** (\u00a7 9A(c)): the fifth anniversary of the date of enactment\n22. **Water-use effectiveness limit and existing-load compliance date** (\u00a7 9A(d)): 0.36 L/kWh, computed as annual site water use divided by annual energy consumed by information-technology equipment; by the third anniversary of the date of enactment\n23. **Sound limits at the nearest dwelling** (\u00a7 9A(e)): 55 dBA day; 45 dBA night, each the A-weighted equivalent continuous sound level over any one hour, with day from 7:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m. and night from 10:00 p.m. to 7:00 a.m.\n24. **Fallback shortfall amount** (\u00a7 9A(f)): [$___ per megawatt-hour]\n25. **Industrial-heat savings computation method** (\u00a7 8(d-1)): [reserved for the drafting committee]\n26. **Clean-materials classes, premiums, and annual volumes** (\u00a7 8(e-1A)): [reserved for the drafting committee]\n27. **Node curtailment threshold** (\u00a7 4(e)): [___ megawatt-hours per year, or ___ percent of the node's clean generation]\n28. **Rating update horizon** (\u00a7 4A(a)): [___ hours ahead]\n29. **Congestion-cost threshold for measured-condition ratings, and the period to implement** (\u00a7 4A(b)): [$___ per line per year; ___ months]\n30. **Flexible-interconnection curtailment fraction** (\u00a7 6(g)): [___ percent of annual output]\n31. **Customer data period and revocation period** (\u00a7 11(b)): [___ days; ___ days]\n32. **Distribution-facility solicitation threshold** (\u00a7 11A(a)): [$___]\n33. **Near-term constraint period** (\u00a7 11A(e)): [___ months]\n34. **Section 4A(a) and \u00a7 11(b) compliance periods** (\u00a7 17(c)): [___ months after enactment]\n35. **Hours at or above rating, where congestion cost is not computed** (\u00a7 4A(b)): [___ hours per year]\n36. **Section 11A onset period** (\u00a7 17(c)): [___ months after enactment]"}],"asOf":"2026-11-09"}}}};
